---

<Base
  title="List"
  description="List: semantic collection of rows (ul/ol) with real anatomy - leading, content, trailing - dividers, interactive rows and order."
>
  <h1>List</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A ready <strong> with UI</strong>, not a <code>&lt;li&gt;</code> with typography. Below is a
    real <code>&lt;ul&gt;</code> or <code>&lt;ol&gt;</code> —the reader hears a list of N items—, and
    Above it provides the row anatomy: <strong>leading</strong> (icon, avatar, number),
    <strong>content</strong> (title and description) and <strong>trailing</strong> (meta, badge,
    action), with dividers. It is composable by parts and functional: a row that acts is a
    <code>&lt;a&gt;</code>/<code>&lt;button&gt;</code> real, never a <code>onClick</code> in the
    <code>&lt;li&gt;</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>The line, one slot at a time</h2>
  <p>
    The six examples that follow are <strong>the same list</strong>, and each adds exactly
    a piece on the previous one. They are read in order: what appears again at each step is the only
    that changed in the markup.
  </p>

  <h3>1 · List only</h3>
  <p>
    The floor: a real <code>&lt;ul&gt;</code> with rows and dividers, without a single slot. There is already height
    of row, inset and hairlines, and the reader hears <em>list of 5 items</em>. Many lists do not need
    nothing more than this.
  </p>
  <Showcase code={plainHtml} react={plainReact} lang="html" label="1 · List only" note="ul + item">
    <div class="sk-list-demo"><Fragment set:html={plainHtml} /></div>
  </Showcase>

  <h3>2 · Title and description</h3>
  <p>
    Enter <code>sk-list__content</code>, the flexing spine. The title and description are
    stack, and it is the only part of the row that shrinks: that is why a long title cuts there and does not
    pushes the row wider than its container.
  </p>
  <Showcase code={titledHtml} react={titledReact} lang="html" label="2 · Title and description" note="+ content">
    <div class="sk-list-demo"><Fragment set:html={titledHtml} /></div>
  </Showcase>

  <h3>3 · Average at the beginning</h3>
  <p>
    Enter <code>sk-list__leading</code>: an icon, avatar, or number. It is
    <strong>decorative</strong>, does not name the row — it is the title that names it. An empty slot does not
    exists for the layout, so rows without an icon do not carry ghost padding.
  </p>
  <Showcase code={leadingHtml} react={leadingReact} lang="html" label="3 · Average at the beginning" note="+ leading (Icon)">
    <div class="sk-list-demo"><Fragment set:html={leadingHtml} /></div>
  </Showcase>

  <h3>4 · Goal at the end</h3>
  <p>
    Enter <code>sk-list__trailing</code> and the row is complete. It is anchored at the end with
    <code>margin-inline-start: auto</code> and brings <code>tabular-nums</code>, so the figures of
    Different rows are aligned in a column. Accepts text or a component, such as a Badge.
  </p>
  <Showcase code={trailingHtml} react={trailingReact} lang="html" label="4 · Goal at the end" note="+ trailing + Badge">
    <div class="sk-list-demo"><Fragment set:html={trailingHtml} /></div>
  </Showcase>

  <h3>5 · The row acts</h3>
  <p>
    Same anatomy, now wrapped in a real <code>a[href]</code> with
    <code>sk-list__action sk-interactive</code>. Focus and keyboard are native, and hover, focus and
    press come from <a href="/en/state-layer">state layer</a>. The <code>&lt;li&gt;</code> becomes
    only the one with the divider.
  </p>
  <Showcase code={linksHtml} react={linksReact} lang="html" label="5 · The row acts" note="+ a[href] + state layer">
    <div class="sk-list-demo"><Fragment set:html={linksHtml} /></div>
  </Showcase>

  <h3>6 · Todo junto</h3>
  <p>
    The roof of the staircase: interactive rows, two pieces in the trailing, density
    <code>compact</code> and a disabled row. Nothing new about the component, just the old
    combined.
  </p>
  <Showcase code={fullHtml} react={fullReact} lang="html" label="6 · Todo junto" note="+ density + fila deshabilitada">
    <div class="sk-list-demo"><Fragment set:html={fullHtml} /></div>
  </Showcase>

  <h2>Install</h2>
  <CodeBlock code={install} label="terminal" />
  <CodeBlock code={styles} label="CSS" />

  <h2>Contrato</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Root: <code>&lt;ul class="sk-list"&gt;</code> or <code>&lt;ol&gt;</code>. Each row is a
      <code>&lt;li class="sk-list__item"&gt;</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Row anatomy: <code>sk-list__leading</code>, <code>sk-list__content</code> (with
      <code>sk-list__title</code> and <code>sk-list__description</code>) and
      <code>sk-list__trailing</code>. All optional; compose the ones you use.
    </li>
    <li>
      Instruction Sequences: Use <a href="/en/components/process-list">ProcessList</a>, which
      preserves a <code>&lt;ol&gt;</code> and has the markers, connectors and content of each step.
    </li>
    <li>
      Functional row: a <code>&lt;a&gt;</code>/<code>&lt;button&gt;</code> con
      <code>sk-list__action sk-interactive</code> inside the <code>&lt;li&gt;</code>. Llena la fila y
      hereda foco, teclado y state layer.
    </li>
    <li>
      Persistent state: none. List does not model current selection or destination; for navigation
      active uses <a href="/en/nav-list">Nav list</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Variantes: <code>data-density="compact"</code> y <code>data-dividers="none"</code>.
    </li>
    <li>Without initialization: the behavior is <code>&lt;a&gt;</code>/<code>&lt;button&gt;</code> nativo.</li>
  </ul>

  <StylingHooks
    groups={[
      {
        file: "components/list.css",
        probe: "sk-list",
      },
    ]}
  />
</Base>

<style is:global>
  /* A list is sized by its host: no width, the stage is flex and each `<ul>` se encoge al ancho de
     su fila más larga, así que los seis ejemplos salían de anchos distintos y el paso 1 quedaba en
     77px. El demo le presta el ancho que tendría una columna real; el código enseña el markup sin
     ese ancho, que es del consumidor.
     `is:global`: el HTML autorado vive dentro del srcdoc y no lleva el atributo de scope de Astro. */
  .sk-list-demo {
    inline-size: min(100%, 34rem);
  }
</style>
